    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "What the existing telemetry can tell us",
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Available:** governor temperatures/targets/origins, connection-manager states, lifecycle events, discovery/churn, selected-chain activity and density, rollbacks, validation timings, mempool, ledger population, snapshots, our own forging and KES.\n\n**Not exported by the current nodes:** adopted-block body size and transaction count, producing-pool identity for each received block, per-peer RTT/throughput, and a graph of all competing branches (tines). There are no placeholder charts for these. Network event logs offer a local view, not a network-wide topology.\n\nThe existing singleton block-fetch latency histogram can have **zero observations while the range-fetch path is active**. ChainSync round duration includes awaiting a peer reply and is not RTT. Event-driven gauges can remain unchanged between events; a fresh scrape timestamp does not prove fresh underlying observations."
      },
      "id": 22,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 6,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 43
      }
